 \begin{abstract}
-Mine escape routes are fixed in advance, usually as the shortest way to a shaft, but when water breaks in, the alarm reports only which zone of the mine is flooding. We ask what a fixed escape plan needs to know about where the water enters. On a 248-km tunnel network from a real coal mine, we simulate a flood from each of its 1,587 junctions and compare three plans: the shortest route, a simple rule that avoids the alarm zone, and the shortest route that is guaranteed for every possible source in the zone, which we compute exactly. Three findings stand out. First, shortest routes fail not because the warning is late but because they converge on the shafts, where the water arrives first; with four alarm zones and a 30-minute delay, they can be guaranteed for only 59\% of (worker, flood) pairs. Second, the shortest route that survives every possible source in the zone raises this to 79\%; a simple rule that steers clear of the alarm zone, without checking the flood, reaches 69\% but with much longer walks. Third, almost all of the remaining gap to the 99.8\% possible with the exact source is a lack of information, not of time: finer alarm zones close it, and drawing zone boundaries through the shafts is worth about as much as doubling the number of zones. The results rest on one mine and an uncalibrated flood model.
